WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.625 --> 00:00:01.835
Everyone loves a hero story.

00:00:01.918 --> 00:00:03.962
It's satisfying to
see good overcome evil

00:00:04.045 --> 00:00:06.339
and then ride off
blissfully into the sunset.

00:00:06.423 --> 00:00:08.717
But there's a long history
of stories with heroes

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:11.219
who don't get their
happily ever after.

00:00:11.302 --> 00:00:12.637
According to Aristotle,

00:00:12.721 --> 00:00:15.640
the function of these so-called
tragic heroes was to provide

00:00:15.724 --> 00:00:18.685
a catharsis or a release of
emotions for the audience

00:00:18.768 --> 00:00:20.478
when a fatal character flaw

00:00:20.562 --> 00:00:22.814
led to the hero's
untimely demise.

00:00:22.897 --> 00:00:24.733
The tragic hero is
meant to be the face

00:00:24.816 --> 00:00:26.359
of a cautionary tale,

00:00:26.443 --> 00:00:28.611
but Irish folklore offers
an interesting take

00:00:28.695 --> 00:00:31.656
on this classic archetype
in the story of Cu Chulainn,

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:33.908
a gifted warrior who
knew from childhood

00:00:33.992 --> 00:00:36.578
that he would live a
short but legendary life.

00:00:36.661 --> 00:00:39.664
[whimsical music]

00:00:40.832 --> 00:00:43.293
Cu Chulainn is considered by
many to be the greatest hero

00:00:43.376 --> 00:00:44.586
in Irish mythology

00:00:44.669 --> 00:00:47.088
with only one real
rival for the top spot,

00:00:47.172 --> 00:00:49.132
the hunter Fionn mac Cumhaill.

00:00:49.215 --> 00:00:51.384
Most of our knowledge of
these and other figures

00:00:51.468 --> 00:00:53.428
from Celtic folklore
come from records

00:00:53.511 --> 00:00:56.556
taken by Christian monks in
the 11th and 12th centuries.

00:00:56.639 --> 00:00:58.767
Before that, the people
we now call the Celts,

00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:01.478
thanks to ancient Greeks,
were an oral society.

00:01:01.561 --> 00:01:04.189
They passed knowledge from
one generation to the next

00:01:04.272 --> 00:01:06.900
in the form of stories,
poems, or songs

00:01:06.983 --> 00:01:08.985
that were recited
and memorized.

00:01:09.068 --> 00:01:11.362
The Celts were a collection
of culturally similar

00:01:11.446 --> 00:01:14.324
but independent tribes
who traveled from the Alps

00:01:14.407 --> 00:01:18.286
around 1200 BCE and spread
across much of Western Europe

00:01:18.369 --> 00:01:20.747
by the third century BCE.

00:01:20.830 --> 00:01:23.541
Rome conquered much of
Europe two centuries later,

00:01:23.625 --> 00:01:25.835
but the northern parts that
became Ireland, Scotland,

00:01:25.919 --> 00:01:27.837
and Wales remained
mostly unbothered

00:01:27.921 --> 00:01:30.340
for another several
hundred years.

00:01:30.423 --> 00:01:32.383
The monks' records
don't reveal too much

00:01:32.467 --> 00:01:33.843
about Celtic societies,

00:01:33.927 --> 00:01:36.429
and it's impossible to say how
their Christian perspective

00:01:36.513 --> 00:01:38.681
influenced the
writing we do have,

00:01:38.765 --> 00:01:41.142
but we know that the Celts
understood astronomy,

00:01:41.226 --> 00:01:44.354
respected nature, and
valued cattle so highly

00:01:44.437 --> 00:01:45.772
that wars were often
fought as cattle raids

00:01:45.855 --> 00:01:48.942
or one-on-one duels
between champions.

00:01:49.025 --> 00:01:51.402
Remember that because
it will come up later.

00:01:51.486 --> 00:01:54.072
Modern scholars have divided
the stories from these records

00:01:54.155 --> 00:01:57.033
into four roughly
chronological cycles:

00:01:57.116 --> 00:02:00.620
Mythology, Ulster,
Fenian, and Historical.

00:02:00.703 --> 00:02:03.540
Each cycle is full of
really interesting stories,

00:02:03.623 --> 00:02:06.042
and if you want to learn about
some of the Mythology Cycle,

00:02:06.125 --> 00:02:08.795
check out the "Monstrum"
episode on Celtic fairies.

00:02:08.878 --> 00:02:10.046
Woo.

00:02:10.129 --> 00:02:12.423
But here, we'll focus
on the Ulster Cycle,

00:02:12.507 --> 00:02:13.925
which is set around
the time of Christ

00:02:14.008 --> 00:02:16.928
and heavily features the life
of the warrior Cu Chulainn.

00:02:17.011 --> 00:02:19.472
Born with the name Setanta,
anyone could tell

00:02:19.556 --> 00:02:22.559
Cu Chulainn was destined to
be a hero from the beginning

00:02:22.642 --> 00:02:24.769
simply because his
birth was so remarkable.

00:02:24.853 --> 00:02:26.896
Cu Chulainn's mother was
a woman named Deichtine,

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:28.940
the sister
of Conchobar mac Nessa,

00:02:29.023 --> 00:02:30.358
king of Ulster, or Ulaid,

00:02:30.441 --> 00:02:31.943
the region of Northern Ireland

00:02:32.026 --> 00:02:34.571
for which the folkloric
cycle is named.

00:02:34.654 --> 00:02:36.573
Setanta's father was
the sun god Lugh,

00:02:36.656 --> 00:02:38.616
one of the mythical
Tuatha De Danann.

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:41.327
There are multiple versions
of Setanta's birth story.

00:02:41.411 --> 00:02:44.122
In one, Deichtine and her
handmaidens are whisked away

00:02:44.205 --> 00:02:46.916
on the eve of her wedding
to the mystical Otherworld,

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:49.502
where she accidentally
drinks a tiny version of Lugh

00:02:49.586 --> 00:02:50.837
and becomes pregnant.

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:53.631
In another, Cu Chulainn
is conceived thrice.

00:02:53.715 --> 00:02:55.216
At first,
he was born to a woman

00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:56.551
who Deichtine
helped through labor

00:02:56.634 --> 00:02:58.511
thanks to Lugh's interference,

00:02:58.595 --> 00:03:00.847
but he died in childhood
as Deichtine's foster son.

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:04.309
Next, he was conceived
in a dream Deichtine had

00:03:04.392 --> 00:03:07.020
of the sun god Lugh, but
she aborted the pregnancy

00:03:07.103 --> 00:03:09.355
because she was married
to a mortal man.

00:03:09.439 --> 00:03:11.858
Finally, Deichtine became
pregnant by her husband

00:03:11.941 --> 00:03:14.861
and was urged by Lugh to
name the baby boy Setanta.

00:03:14.944 --> 00:03:17.864
When Setanta was
a young boy, around six,

00:03:17.947 --> 00:03:21.200
in most versions of the tale
he earned himself a new name.

00:03:21.284 --> 00:03:22.952
He traveled with
his family to the home

00:03:23.036 --> 00:03:25.246
of the blacksmith
Culann for a feast

00:03:25.330 --> 00:03:26.789
where he was separated
from the group

00:03:26.873 --> 00:03:30.460
and came face-to-face with the
smith's vicious guard hound.

00:03:30.543 --> 00:03:32.712
Despite his youth,
Setanta defeated the hound

00:03:32.795 --> 00:03:33.963
and then promised to act

00:03:34.047 --> 00:03:36.799
as the smith's new
guard dog in recompense.

00:03:36.883 --> 00:03:40.553
Thus Cu Chulainn, or Culann's
Hound, was born again.

00:03:40.637 --> 00:03:42.764
The next year, Cu Chulainn
overheard a prophecy

00:03:42.847 --> 00:03:46.392
spoken by his maternal
grandfather, the Druid Cathbad.

00:03:46.476 --> 00:03:47.852
It said that anyone who
took up arms on that day

00:03:47.936 --> 00:03:51.689
would have eternal fame,
but live a short life.

00:03:51.773 --> 00:03:53.816
Cu Chulainn approached
the king that very day

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:55.860
and asked to begin
his warrior training,

00:03:55.944 --> 00:03:57.612
thus sealing his fate.

00:03:57.695 --> 00:03:59.113
Cu Chulainn was sent to Scotland

00:03:59.197 --> 00:04:01.449
to train with the
renowned fighter Scathach,

00:04:01.532 --> 00:04:04.369
who gifted him his
legendary spear Gae Bulg,

00:04:04.452 --> 00:04:06.329
which could travel
at lightning speeds

00:04:06.412 --> 00:04:09.165
and shoot 30 little
barbs from its tip.

00:04:09.248 --> 00:04:11.709
Cu Chulainn was
naturally strong and fast

00:04:11.793 --> 00:04:14.462
thanks to his divine parentage,
but while he was away

00:04:14.545 --> 00:04:17.799
he really learned the
ways of both war and love.

00:04:17.882 --> 00:04:21.177
He even sired a son, whom he
later accidentally killed,

00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:23.429
by Scathach's daughter
despite the fact

00:04:23.513 --> 00:04:25.932
that he was betroth to
a woman back in Ireland.

00:04:26.015 --> 00:04:27.892
By the time he returned
to his homeland,

00:04:27.976 --> 00:04:29.727
Cu Chulainn was a young man

00:04:29.811 --> 00:04:32.605
often described as short
and notably beardless

00:04:32.689 --> 00:04:33.773
with fuzzy hair.

00:04:33.856 --> 00:04:35.358
Though it was said that
the women of Ulster

00:04:35.441 --> 00:04:37.819
were driven mad
with lust for him.

00:04:37.902 --> 00:04:39.362
As perhaps the most skilled

00:04:39.445 --> 00:04:41.197
of the king's Red
Branch warriors,

00:04:41.281 --> 00:04:42.615
it was Cu Chulainn's
responsibility

00:04:42.699 --> 00:04:45.159
to protect his land and people.

00:04:45.243 --> 00:04:47.662
Whenever his strength,
speed, and training

00:04:47.745 --> 00:04:49.080
weren't enough to defeat a foe,

00:04:49.163 --> 00:04:50.665
Cu Chulainn relied
on his special power

00:04:50.748 --> 00:04:52.458
called the riastrad.

00:04:52.542 --> 00:04:54.293
Much like a Norse berserker,

00:04:54.377 --> 00:04:56.421
Cu Chulainn could
change form in battle.

00:04:56.504 --> 00:04:58.506
He grew to nine feet tall

00:04:58.589 --> 00:05:00.049
and one of his eyes
either bulged

00:05:00.133 --> 00:05:01.759
or rolled back in his head

00:05:01.843 --> 00:05:05.138
and a haze of blood appeared
around his fuzzy mane.

00:05:05.221 --> 00:05:07.890
When Cu Chulainn was in this
state, he was invincible

00:05:07.974 --> 00:05:11.102
and so enraged he couldn't
tell friend from foe.

00:05:11.185 --> 00:05:13.229
No one was safe from his ire,

00:05:13.312 --> 00:05:15.982
and it was nearly impossible
to bring him back to normal.

00:05:16.065 --> 00:05:19.110
Upon returning home from a
particularly violent mission,

00:05:19.193 --> 00:05:21.571
Cu Chulainn, still
gripped by the riastrad,

00:05:21.654 --> 00:05:23.781
demanded to be given
an opponent to fight

00:05:23.865 --> 00:05:27.035
or else he would kill everyone
in the entire fortress.

00:05:27.118 --> 00:05:28.745
Instead of offering up
a victim,

00:05:28.828 --> 00:05:31.789
the leaders of Ulster sent
out a group of naked women.

00:05:31.873 --> 00:05:34.500
Cu Chulainn was so
embarrassed by the sight

00:05:34.584 --> 00:05:35.835
that he averted his gaze,

00:05:35.918 --> 00:05:38.463
giving the other warriors
time to restrain him

00:05:38.546 --> 00:05:40.923
and dunk him in water
until he calmed down.

00:05:41.007 --> 00:05:42.425
The people of
Ulster were grateful

00:05:42.508 --> 00:05:44.260
for Cu Chulainn's
murderous rage, however,

00:05:44.343 --> 00:05:45.636
when their kingdom was invaded

00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:48.473
by the army of Connacht's
warrior queen Medb.

00:05:48.556 --> 00:05:50.516
Remember when Moiya told
you cattle were valuable

00:05:50.600 --> 00:05:52.310
in ancient Celtic societies?

00:05:52.393 --> 00:05:56.147
Well, Medb's master plan was
to amass empire-building wealth

00:05:56.230 --> 00:05:58.274
by stealing Ulster's prize bull.

00:05:58.357 --> 00:05:59.650
On the day of the attack,

00:05:59.734 --> 00:06:02.070
all of the soldiers in
Ulster's army were struck down

00:06:02.153 --> 00:06:04.322
by a curse that gave
them menstrual pangs

00:06:04.405 --> 00:06:05.615
and prevented them
from fighting.

00:06:05.698 --> 00:06:07.366
To that I say two things.

00:06:07.450 --> 00:06:08.826
One, this wouldn't have happened

00:06:08.910 --> 00:06:10.369
if you had more women warriors

00:06:10.453 --> 00:06:12.330
who already knew how to
fight through the pain.

00:06:12.413 --> 00:06:15.375
And two, it was
Cu Chulainn's time to shine.

00:06:15.458 --> 00:06:18.503
In what became known as the
legendary Tain Bo Cuailnge,

00:06:18.586 --> 00:06:20.171
or the Cattle Raid of Cooley,

00:06:20.254 --> 00:06:22.715
17-year-old Cu Chulainn
single-handedly fought off

00:06:22.799 --> 00:06:26.177
Medb's army one soldier
at a time for days

00:06:26.260 --> 00:06:28.096
until the rest of the
Ulster men recovered enough

00:06:28.179 --> 00:06:29.388
to back him up.

00:06:29.472 --> 00:06:30.515
Medb's army was
forced to retreat,

00:06:30.598 --> 00:06:32.475
but it wouldn't be
their last invasion

00:06:32.558 --> 00:06:33.893
of Cu Chulainn's home.

00:06:33.976 --> 00:06:36.104
In a classical tragedy,
this would be about the time

00:06:36.187 --> 00:06:38.606
that Cu Chulainn would be lured
from the path of greatness

00:06:38.689 --> 00:06:39.941
by his fatal flaw.

00:06:40.024 --> 00:06:41.609
He would demonstrate
too much hubris

00:06:41.692 --> 00:06:43.611
or get caught up in a
web of his own lies.

00:06:43.694 --> 00:06:46.572
But instead of a fatal flaw,
it's something called a geis

00:06:46.656 --> 00:06:48.658
that leads to
Cu Chulainn's demise.

00:06:48.741 --> 00:06:51.160
Geasa are taboos, or curses,

00:06:51.244 --> 00:06:53.788
or can even be seen as
gifts bestowed upon people

00:06:53.871 --> 00:06:56.916
by their parents, a Druid, or
some other authority figure.

00:06:56.999 --> 00:07:00.670
To break a geis is to bring
about extreme misfortune.

00:07:00.753 --> 00:07:03.089
Cu Chulainn's behavior was
constrained by two geasa.

00:07:03.172 --> 00:07:05.716
He must accept any food
offered to him by a woman,

00:07:05.800 --> 00:07:07.802
and he must not eat dog meat.

00:07:07.885 --> 00:07:10.388
If you are familiar with
common folklore tropes,

00:07:10.471 --> 00:07:11.764
you might be able to see

00:07:11.848 --> 00:07:13.766
where these unfortunately
contradicting taboos

00:07:13.850 --> 00:07:15.059
will lead our hero.

00:07:15.143 --> 00:07:16.978
When Cu Chulainn
was 27 years old,

00:07:17.061 --> 00:07:19.063
Queen Medb and one
of his other enemies,

00:07:19.147 --> 00:07:22.066
Lugaid mac Con Roi,
invaded Ulster.

00:07:22.150 --> 00:07:24.485
On his way to the battle,
Cu Chulainn was tricked

00:07:24.569 --> 00:07:27.071
into accepting hound meat
from a group of women,

00:07:27.155 --> 00:07:29.699
breaking one geis
to uphold another.

00:07:29.782 --> 00:07:32.076
The strength and power
were immediately sapped

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:34.829
from his body and he was
made vulnerable to weapons.

00:07:34.912 --> 00:07:36.998
Cu Chulainn was mortally
wounded in that battle

00:07:37.081 --> 00:07:38.666
by one of Lugaid's spears,

00:07:38.749 --> 00:07:40.334
but he didn't go
down without a fight.

00:07:40.418 --> 00:07:43.629
In one last heroic move,
Cu Chulainn tied himself

00:07:43.713 --> 00:07:46.716
to a standing stone and
faced down his opponents.

00:07:46.799 --> 00:07:49.802
Even in death, his reputation
kept the other army at bay

00:07:49.886 --> 00:07:51.512
for more than a day

00:07:51.596 --> 00:07:54.557
until a raven landed on his
body and broke the illusion.

00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:57.476
Cu Chulainn likely wasn't
based on a real person,

00:07:57.560 --> 00:08:00.104
but the legendary figure
continues to inspire

00:08:00.188 --> 00:08:02.440
and influence in
modern-day Ireland.

00:08:02.523 --> 00:08:04.775
You can find murals and
statues dedicated to him

00:08:04.859 --> 00:08:06.110
around the country,

00:08:06.194 --> 00:08:08.154
and references to him
can be found in media

00:08:08.237 --> 00:08:09.530
around the world,

00:08:09.614 --> 00:08:11.824
like the popular myth-based
video game "SMITE."

00:08:11.908 --> 00:08:14.827
In classical literature,
according to Aristotle,

00:08:14.911 --> 00:08:17.413
tragic heroes were defined
by their fatal flaw

00:08:17.496 --> 00:08:18.539
and untimely death.

00:08:18.623 --> 00:08:20.791
One could argue that
Cu Chulainn fits this mold

00:08:20.875 --> 00:08:22.793
and that his fatal flaws
were the hubris,

00:08:22.877 --> 00:08:24.795
impulsivity, and desire for fame

00:08:24.879 --> 00:08:26.797
that led him to fulfill
Cathbad's prophecy

00:08:26.881 --> 00:08:28.341
at the age of seven.

00:08:28.424 --> 00:08:31.219
(host 2)
But Cu Chulainn's death
wasn't too early at all.

00:08:31.302 --> 00:08:33.387
He always knew his
days were numbered,

00:08:33.471 --> 00:08:36.098
and he got exactly
the destiny he wanted.

00:08:36.182 --> 00:08:38.559
He lived the rough-and-tumble
life of a warrior,

00:08:38.643 --> 00:08:41.479
built a reputation as
a truly fearsome opponent,

00:08:41.562 --> 00:08:43.689
and continued to live on
in national memory

00:08:43.773 --> 00:08:45.524
for thousands of years.

00:08:45.608 --> 00:08:48.986
Sorry Aristotle, but that
doesn't sound very tragic to me.

00:08:49.070 --> 00:08:52.114
Accessibility provided by the
U.S. Department of Education.

